WA-2025-SB5912.summary:
The Indigent Defense Task Force is reinstated and will study Washington's current system for providing legal representation to indigent defendants. The task force will report findings to the Governor, the Washington Supreme Court, and the appropriate committees of the Legislature by January 1, 2028.

The task force's report will:
• describe and compare the indigent defense delivery models used in different regions of the state;
• evaluate the progress made by jurisdictions toward compliance with adopted indigent defense caseload standards;
• identify barriers that impede compliance with adopted caseload standards and recommend specific corrective actions or technical assistance;
• examine delays, case backlogs, and other system impacts associated with excessive caseloads, staffing shortages, and workforce constraints;
• assess workforce recruitment, retention, supervision, and training needs, with particular attention toward rural, underserved, and high-turnover jurisdictions;
• review alternative indigent defense delivery systems currently in use in Washington, as well as models that have been proposed to address caseload, staffing, or geographic challenges;
• identify system-level practices that may reduce unnecessary case filings and associated indigent defense workloads while maintaining public safety and due process;
• analyze existing funding sources and assess whether current funding mechanisms are sufficient and sustainable under the adopted caseload standards;
• review available state and local indigent defense data sets and make recommendations to improve indigent defense data collection; and
• make recommendations for changes to indigent defense delivery systems necessary to ensure constitutionally adequate representation that is effective, timely, and financially sustainable statewide.

The task force will consist of:
• one member appointed by the governor;
• the director of the Office of Financial Management or their designee;
• the director of the Office of Public Defense or their designee;
• three members appointed by the chief justice of the Washington Supreme Court, one who is a representative from the superior court judges' association and one who is a representative from the district and municipal court judges' association;
• two members appointed by the Washington State Bar Association, at least one of whom is a current provider of indigent criminal defense representation;
• two members appointed by the Washington State Association of Counties, one from Eastern Washington and one from Western Washington;
• one member appointed by the Association of Washington Cities;
• one member appointed by the Washington Association of Prosecuting Attorneys;
• three members from underrepresented populations who have direct lived experience with indigent defense services;
